# GDM
The one your roadmap actually wants: GDM on the free energy A = E − TS over orbitals and occupations — ensemble-DFT direct minimization (Marzari–Vanderbilt–Payne 1997; Freysoldt–Boeck–Neugebauer 2009).

# rho fitting:
Metric ≠ span. Orthonormality means S = I, so the normal equations collapse to c = ⟨f|ρ⟩. That's about cost and conditioning — no solve. Whether ρ̃ = ρ is a completely separate question about whether ρ lies in span{G}, and orthonormality says nothing about it. "The projection IS the fit" is only a statement about (1); I let it carry (2) as well.

And the answer to (2) splits by lineage. Your intuition is half-right in a way I find more interesting than either "exact" or "not exact":

Pure PW orbitals: genuinely exact. ρ = ψ*ψ is exactly band-limited to the difference set {G_i − G_j}, since a product of two plane waves is a plane wave. The codebase already knows this and builds for it — PlaneWave_IBS.C:112 creates the CD fit basis at Ecut()*std::max(4.0, mp.relCutoff), and its comment states the rule: |G_i−G_j| ≤ 2 max|G|, so the density needs 4× the orbital cutoff. So ρ̃ = ρ holds by construction of the cutoff, not by luck.
GPW (Gaussian orbitals): not exact, and never can be. A Gaussian product has infinite bandwidth, so a finite {G} ball truncates it. That's precisely what ReportGridCharge / GridChargeLost measures — CP2K's "Electronic density on regular grids" line. So GPW's Hartree is a genuine approximation that converges only as the density cutoff grows.

# Ball vs AliasFree
1) BallOnly vs AliasFree — same ball, different quadrature grid
The crucial thing: both keep the identical ball of G-vectors (the physics — the {G} the density and fit live in). What differs is only the real-space FFT grid used as the quadrature engine to compute matrix elements and collocate.

Here's the mechanism. A matrix element ⟨G|f|G'⟩ involves a product of two ball waves, so it has Fourier content across the difference set {G−G'} — which spans up to twice the ball radius (2m, if the ball reaches component m). To sample that product exactly, the grid has to resolve up to 2m:

AliasFree: divisions N = 4m+1. Resolves the full difference set → every ⟨G|f|G'⟩ is an exact quadrature. Discretization error then lives only in the ball radius (Ecut), never in N.
BallOnly: divisions N = 2m+1. Resolves the ball alone. The products of two ball waves run up to 2m but the grid's Nyquist is only m, so the top half aliases (folds back) — accepted as Ecut-controlled error. ~8× fewer points (2× per dimension, cubed). This is CP2K's bet.
Your intuition flipped: more grid points = higher Nyquist = less aliasing. The doubling isn't causing aliasing — it's what removes it. "AliasFree" names the outcome (no fold-back), achieved by the larger grid. BallOnly is the smaller grid that aliases. So 4m+1 (double) is the alias-free one, exactly as the name says.

And the punchline that ties back to your GDM hunt (enum doc, last two lines): under the raw collocated XC feed, the pointwise ρ samples are analytically exact on any raster — so BallOnly's aliasing touches only quadrature/Poisson accuracy, not the density itself and (via the raw adjoint) not variationality. That's why production ships BallOnly and still converges cleanly.

2) applyRaw / applyRawAdjoint — a matched exact-transpose pair
These are the two closures on the G_ERI3 tensor (src/BasisSet/Internal/GMap.C:74–79):

applyRaw(D) = forward: collocate the density matrix onto the real-space grid, ρ(r) = φᵀDφ = Σ_ij D_ij χ_i(r)χ_j(r), done analytically on compact Gaussian boxes across the multi-grid ladder (finest level kept raw, coarser levels zero-padded/combined spectrally — no ball restriction anywhere). "Raw" = the actual density, not projected onto any fit basis. Because D is PSD, ρ ≥ 0 pointwise (to screening precision) — unlike the ball-fit route, whose Gibbs lobes ring negative on sharp products.
applyRawAdjoint(v) = backward, and the key word is exact adjoint (matrix transpose) of applyRaw: take a real-space field v(r) on the same grid → assemble ⟨i|v|j⟩ = Σ_r w_r v(r) χ_i(r)χ_j(r) (per-level box-truncation — literally the transpose of the zero-pad — then an analytic per-pair gather).
Why that makes it variational: because applyRawAdjoint is the literal transpose of applyRaw, the discrete adjoint identity ⟨applyRaw(D), v⟩ = ⟨D, applyRawAdjoint(v)⟩ holds exactly. So with E_xc = Σ_r w_r ε_xc(ρ_r)ρ_r and ρ = applyRaw(D):

